{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgSk6JkSCVo9GPneuEvPe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Hypothesis:** The reminder code computes the renewal date wrongly in three ways. Two of them match your two customer reports.\n\n**System:** Reproduced. I ran `reminder_date` on the rows in `customers.csv`:\n\n| Customer | signup | annual_since | Code says renewal | Code says reminder |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| C-311 | 2025-11-02 | 2026-03-10 | 2026-11-02 | **2026-10-19** (matches their report) |\n| C-327 | 2023-06-01 | 2023-06-01 | 2024-05-31 | **2024-05-17** (matches their report) |\n\n**Findings (all from running the current code):**\n1. **It uses the wrong start date (C-311).** `renewal_date` in `reminders.py` reads `signup` and never reads `annual_since`. C-311 signed up in November 2025 and went annual on 2026-03-10. The code treats them as renewing on the signup anniversary. If their annual term runs from 2026-03-10, the renewal is 2027-03-10 and the reminder should be 2027-02-24. They got a reminder about 4.5 months early, for a renewal that is not theirs.\n2. **It adds 365 days instead of one calendar year (C-327).** 2023-06-01 plus 365 days is 2024-05-31, because Feb 29, 2024 falls in between. The real anniversary is June 1, so the reminder should be May 18. The code sends it on May 17, which is 15 days before June 1. The error is one day in any span that crosses a Feb 29. The customer's \"not two weeks\" is correct, but the gap is small.\n3. **It only ever computes the first renewal (not reported yet).** The date is always `start + 365`, with no step to the next anniversary. For any customer past their first term, the reminder date is in the past. C-302 (signup 2024-08-15) gets 2025-08-01 forever, and it is now 2026-10-27. C-327's date is stuck in 2024. Whether those customers get reminders depends on the nightly job. The job is not in this repo, so I could not check how it picks customers. If it matches on `reminder_date == today`, long-standing annual customers get no reminder at all. This is a lead, not a confirmed defect.\n\n**Why the test passes:** `test_two_weeks_before` covers only C-340. For C-340, `signup` equals `annual_since`, it is the first term, and the span has no Feb 29. All three bugs are hidden by that one case.\n\n**Decide:**\n1. **Start date.** Does the annual term run from `annual_since`? My default is yes. What does `signup` mean for customers whose plan changed?\n2. **Anniversary rule.** Use the same calendar date each year (my default). What should Feb 29 starts renew on: Feb 28 or Mar 1?\n3. **Repeat years.** Should the date be the next anniversary on or after today? My default is yes. Or does the nightly job keep its own state?\n\n**Scope:**\n- In: `renewal_date` and `reminder_date`.\n- Out: the nightly job, billing, and any resend to C-311 and C-327.\n\n**Risk:** The change affects customer emails, so a wrong rule sends reminders on the wrong day. It is easy to undo, because it is only date logic. It could cause a one-time burst of reminders for long-standing customers once the date moves forward. Check the nightly job before landing so it does not double-send.\n\n**Claims** (each with its falsifier):\n- **C1 (introduce):** The renewal is based on `annual_since`. Example: C-311 gets reminder 2027-02-24. Falsifier: billing shows C-311's renewal is really on the signup anniversary.\n- **C2 (introduce):** The renewal falls on the same calendar date each year. Example: C-327's first renewal is 2024-06-01 and the reminder is 2024-05-18. Falsifier: billing charges every 365 days exactly.\n- **C3 (introduce):** The reminder date always points to an upcoming renewal. Example: C-302 on 2026-10-27 shows renewal 2027-08-15 and reminder 2027-08-01. Falsifier: the job tracks past renewals itself and the old dates are never used.\n- **C4 (preserve):** The reminder is exactly 14 days before the renewal. C-340 stays at 2026-01-06.\n\n**Not explored:** The nightly job, how billing defines the renewal date, how many customers are affected (only 4 rows in the export), and the Feb 29 edge. I changed no code.\n\nVote GO, NO-GO, or HOLD."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":183,"cache_read_input_tokens":40396,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":183},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff3b5f85-57e5-4796-9cce-c93d2e264219","uuid":"9ba893b7-cb75-4ba5-a4fe-078efb579936","timestamp":"2026-10-27T12:30:15.379Z","request_id":"req_011CgSk6JVoV5WbkM4EYMsbk"}
